"Organizes business processes into increasingly specific categories so that teams can share scope, terminology and ownership.\n\nUse when: Create a common enterprise process inventory. Identify gaps, overlaps and comparable activities across departments.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need execution sequence or an individual case's decision logic.\n\nVisual grammar: Parent-child nesting denotes classification rather than task order. Stable identifiers connect categories, process groups, processes and activities. Consistent levels distinguish comparable units of work.\n\nBuild: Select a reference framework and define the enterprise boundary. Classify existing work under the appropriate categories and levels. Assign local definitions and owners while recording intentional framework deviations.\n\nCheck: Children describe parts or refinements of their parent scope. Peer elements use comparable granularity and distinct definitions. The hierarchy does not silently imply execution dependencies.\n\nPitfalls: Copying a reference framework without checking actual applicability. Duplicating the same process under multiple departments without references.\n\nExample: A manufacturer classifies manage-suppliers, source-materials and receive-goods under procurement, with local owners and shared process identifiers.\n\nAlternatives: IDEF0 function model, Work breakdown structure."
